{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791059388,"nonce":"DFzS2fU7si/IUS2UzeKzqJTXbQU=","title":"CSS state watches outlive the elements they watch","message":"ashui.css.Css.dependOn makes one Watch per (node, state) that a selector tests (:hover etc.), on that node's Interaction signal, and keeps a list of the identities to re-match. Neither is released when the node is removed: the Watch, the Interaction's signal and the list stay alive, and the list keeps forgotten identities (flush skips them, so behaviour is right, only memory grows). A UI that keeps making and removing elements matched by state rules (a \u003cfor\u003e list with .row:hover) grows without bound. Fix: release a node's watches when its identity is forgotten (Identity.forget/forgetSubtree), and drop forgotten identities from the dependency lists, or own each Watch under the element's Owner.","files":null}]}